{"entity": "openUBMC-Switch", "type": "artifact", "description": "openUBMC-Switch is a sample value for SoftwareName used in switch environments."}
{"entity": "BMC", "type": "location", "description": "BMC, or Baseboard Management Controller, is a dedicated microcontroller embedded in server motherboards and hardware platforms, serving as a specialized subsystem for monitoring, managing, and maintaining system health and hardware operations. It enables out-of-band management, allowing remote administration of servers even when the primary operating system is offline or unresponsive. Acting as the host in the PECI (Processor Event Call Interface) bus architecture, the BMC directly communicates with the processor to monitor CPU states and temperature metrics. It performs real-time monitoring of environmental and physical conditions, including power, temperature, voltage, and hardware events, supporting remote diagnostics, system recovery, boot coordination, and maintenance through standardized protocols such as IPMI (Intelligent Platform Management Interface).\n\nThe BMC provides comprehensive hardware monitoring, including M.2 drive attributes such as presence, capacity, health, temperature, and remaining life, as well as SSD and NVMe drive health metrics via the NVMe-MI (NVMe Management Interface) protocol. It plays a central role in system reliability by delivering continuous telemetry, event handling, and time initialization services, including synchronization with the Network Time Protocol (NTP). The BMC coordinates firmware and hardware upgrade processes, including CPLD (Complex Programmable Logic Device) upgrades, using mechanisms like \\\"无感升级\\\" (silent upgrade) to ensure seamless, low-impact transitions between firmware versions. It also supports full-package firmware upgrades to prevent version mismatches and enhance system integrity.\n\nIn addition to monitoring and upgrading, the BMC manages system configuration, handles BIOS-related requests such as access to startup status registers, and provides silkscreen information. It supports a range of network functions, including responding to M-SEARCH requests and processing BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection) packets, while acting as a centralized management component responsible for data synchronization, network traffic processing, and enforcing security policies such as user authentication and login controls. The BMC interfaces with consumer modules and other system components via register operations and supports software configuration through the CSR configuration dictionary, where it is treated as a class for software management.\n\nThe BMC is a critical component in observability architectures, serving as a source of telemetry data transmitted to the OpenTelemetry Collector via the OTLP (OpenTelemetry Protocol) over a unidirectional channel by default, with support for secure communication enhanced by two-factor authentication requiring prior import of both root and client certificates. It is involved in the configuration and upgrade of the White Label Package and is associated with running specific firmware, such as openUBMC firmware, which enables system monitoring and management capabilities. While fundamentally a hardware-based microcontroller, the BMC serves as a critical coordinator between firmware, software, and system components, ensuring the operability, security, and manageability of server platforms across their lifecycle. Some descriptions also indicate support for firmware updates via JTAG and execution of VME (VersaModule Eurocard) file operations, highlighting its role in low-level system control and configuration.<SEP>BMC is a system component that supports CPLD upgrades through SVF file parsing and JTAG signal control.<SEP>BMC (Baseboard Management Controller) is a specialized microcontroller used for monitoring and managing system health and firmware upgrades.<SEP>BMC is a Baseboard Management Controller responsible for monitoring and managing system health and network functions.<SEP>BMC (Baseboard Management Controller) is a dedicated controller used for monitoring and managing server systems, especially network functions via NCSI.<SEP>BMC is the baseboard management controller, the system on which the Storage component and its dependencies are deployed.<SEP>BMC is a component responsible for initiating the hardware.service and starting the storage component during system startup."}
