{"entity": "SlotID", "type": "attribute", "description": "SlotID is a standardized identifier used within PCIe (Peripheral Component Interconnect Express) systems to represent the physical slot where a hardware component, such as a PCIe card, is installed. It serves as a unique reference for the physical location of a device within a system chassis, enabling precise mapping and identification of hardware positions. SlotID is typically implemented as a U8 (8-bit) or U32 (32-bit) value, depending on the system’s implementation and data model, with the U32 representation allowing for expanded slot addressing in larger or more complex topologies. While often optional in the PCIeDevice class, SlotID is a critical attribute for infrastructure management and device enumeration, particularly when associating devices with their physical locations.\n\nThe identifier is commonly used to map to PcieAddrInfo records, facilitating system-level tracking and configuration. It denotes the physical slot number within the system and is designed to correspond directly to the actual slot position in the chassis, supporting accurate hardware inventory, fault diagnosis, and system provisioning. In some implementations, SlotID is implemented via variable substitution mechanisms, allowing dynamic resolution of the physical slot number during system configuration or runtime operations. As a core field in PCIe topology management, SlotID ensures consistent identification and referencing of PCIe devices, regardless of system configuration or deployment context. The collective descriptions confirm that SlotID is uniformly defined as a physical slot identifier, emphasizing its role in accurately locating PCIe hardware within the system framework.<SEP>SlotID is a variable representing the physical slot location of a PCIe card in the system chassis.<SEP>SlotID is a required unsigned 8-bit integer attribute indicating the slot number used for PCIeCard positioning."}
