{"entity": "Virtual Machine", "type": "environment", "description": "Virtual Machine refers to a software-based emulation of a physical computer system, designed to simulate a complete computing environment within a host machine. This emulated environment enables the execution of one or more operating systems—such as openEuler, CentOS 7.9, openEuler 20.03 SP4, openEuler 22.03 SP3, openEuler 23.09, openEuler 24.03, and openEuler 24.03-LTS-SP1—as well as applications, in isolated and secure environments. Virtual Machines are commonly deployed using hypervisors such as QEMU, VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels Desktop, and Azure, and can be managed through platforms like Aliyun's ECS service or H3C’s hyper-converged infrastructure. These environments are widely used for testing, development, system upgrades, and migration processes, including kernel hot upgrades with minimal interruption (e.g., 7-second downtime). Specific instances involve the use of virtual machines to run the openEuler operating system, troubleshoot issues like Zabbix-Agent malfunctions or boot anomalies after unexpected shutdowns, configure network settings (e.g., NAT or additional network cards), and conduct experiments with various OS versions such as openEuler 20.03-sp1. Some virtual machines are instantiated from qcow2 disk images, hosted on physical machines like the 2288H V5 or Tian Gong Server TG225 B1, and may be part of system environments managed by Microsoft Corporation or Sysmaster version 0.5. The virtual machine environment is particularly recommended for installing software like x2openEuler to minimize deployment risks on production servers. Overall, it functions as a flexible, scalable, and isolated computing platform, capable of hosting diverse configurations of CPU, memory, and operating systems, making it indispensable for system testing, development, and infrastructure management."}
