{"entity": "openUBMC", "type": "organization", "description": "openUBMC is an open-source project and community initiative primarily focused on the development and management of Baseboard Management Controller (BMC) firmware and related hardware technologies. It serves as a comprehensive open-source BMC implementation platform, emphasizing system monitoring, hardware control, and firmware management in server environments. The project supports advanced capabilities such as CPLD zero-downtime upgrade (CPLD无感升级), enabling high-availability operations with minimal service disruption. It integrates with the Redfish standards and includes telemetry services to enhance real-time system monitoring, while also offering SNMP functionality, MIB files, and support for parsing SMBIOS fields including Processor Information, Cache Information, Physical Memory Array, and Memory Device structures. A key component of the platform is the usb_entry module, which facilitates USB near-end operational functions.\n\nThe project is actively integrated within the openEuler ecosystem, contributing to documentation production mechanisms, development guidelines, and the advancement of Doc Tools. It supports northbound interface configurations through a multi-layered customization mechanism, facilitating product lifecycle management, interface customization, and cross-ecosystem collaboration. openUBMC also plays a role in software distribution, with the term \"openUBMC\" used as a suffix in rootfs image and archive names, reflecting its presence in firmware and system software releases.\n\nWhile multiple descriptions emphasize openUBMC as an open-source community centered on BMC software development, it is also recognized as a technical entity within industry standards, notably appearing as a value for the BoardManufacturer property and as a sample value for SoftwareName and PackageName attributes. Despite some conflicting references to its nature — including as a website, platform, or project — these functions coalesce into a unified open-source ecosystem focused on BMC-based system management.\n\nFurthermore, openUBMC is involved in storage management, supporting NVMe and SATA protocols with dedicated hardware abstraction layers. The platform facilitates NVMe-MI and SFF VPD protocol support for storage device communication and monitoring. It enables monitoring of SSD health metrics and includes default handling for NVMe and SATA storage devices, positioning it as a project for BMC-based storage management with an emphasis on system monitoring and hardware abstraction. The entity is recognized across technical domains as a provider of open-source firmware, hardware resources, comprehensive documentation, and development tools, contributing significantly to broader initiatives in open-source hardware and firmware innovation.<SEP>openUBMC is an open-source BMC platform that supports VME-based firmware upgrade capabilities through API integration.<SEP>openUBMC is an open-source project that includes the Storage Component for managing storage resources.<SEP>openUBMC is an open-source project providing tools and frameworks for development environments."}
{"entity": "Upgrade Failure", "type": "event", "description": "Upgrade Failure refers to the unsuccessful completion of a system upgrade process, representing the condition that triggers the rollback mechanism when the intended migration does not proceed as expected. This outcome occurs when key system checks—such as operating system compatibility—are not satisfied, or when unresolved software conflicts prevent progress. Specifically, Upgrade Failure has been documented in multiple contexts, including attempts to upgrade from CentOS 7.9 to OpenEuler 22.03 SP1 and from OpenEuler 22.03 SP1 to SP4. In each case, the failure stems from technical obstacles, such as dependency issues, package conflicts, and missing commands during critical stages of the process—most notably during the third step of the upgrade workflow. The failure is not limited to a single phase but encompasses the entire upgrade lifecycle, indicating that the system cannot successfully transition from the base operating system to the target version due to persistent integration challenges. The event is characterized by an inability to complete migration tasks, resulting in a non-functional or unstable state that necessitates intervention. While the core nature of Upgrade Failure remains consistent—its manifestation as an unsuccessful upgrade process—it is observed in distinct technical scenarios involving different source and target OS versions, suggesting variations in root causes across deployments. Nonetheless, the overarching theme is the breakdown in system consistency due to unresolved dependencies and compatibility barriers.<SEP>Upgrade Failure is an event that occurs when a firmware update process fails due to missing configuration, key mismatch, or certificate issues."}
