{"entity": "x2openEuler", "type": "method", "description": "x2openEuler is an open-source system migration and upgrade tool designed to facilitate the transition from various Linux distributions—particularly CentOS 5, CentOS 6, CentOS 7.5, and CentOS 7.9—to openEuler. It serves as a comprehensive framework supporting both in-place and batch upgrades, with specific focus on migration to openEuler 20.03 LTS SP1, openEuler 22.03-LTS, and openEuler 22.03-LTS SP3. The tool is also used to upgrade older versions of openEuler to newer releases, making it a key asset in the openEuler ecosystem for system lifecycle management. Developed and maintained as part of the openEuler community, x2openEuler functions as a migration suite that includes environment checks, compatibility assessments, configuration analysis, and automated scripting to streamline the upgrade process.\n\nThe platform is accessible via a web-based portal interface, typically located at `/usr/local/x2openEuler/portal`, and requires proper service startup for full functionality. It features a backend management system with a database structure for tracking tasks, nodes, and upgrade progress. Implementations involve deployment on target systems, with components installed in designated directories such as `/usr/local/x2openEuler/portal/service`. The tool supports package management, repository source configuration, and white list verification, and is referenced in contexts involving YUM/DNF repositories, system logs, and dependency checks—such as issues with `glibc-header` or conflicts with MySQL and MariaDB during installation.\n\nx2openEuler is associated with several technical challenges during use, including SSH connectivity failures, repository metadata detection errors, directory mounting issues, and unintended modifications to system configuration files. Specific versioning includes v2.0.0 and v3.0.0-20231230, with subsequent updates like v3.0.0-2024930 and v20240818 identified in logs. Known bugs include SSDH service interruption post-upgrade and limitations in task creation. The tool is also linked to operational tasks such as whitelist validation, hardware compatibility assessment, and integration with OpenEuler’s ecosystem for synchronization of system configurations and service statuses.\n\nWhile primarily tied to CentOS migration, x2openEuler has been used to transition from other distributions such as Oracle Linux, Ubuntu, BCLinux, and Red Hat. It also supports upgrades to BC-Linux for Euler 21.10 and has been tested on systems like Red Hat 7.9 and 8.6. The tool is distributed via RPM packages and involves initialization scripts, with the system requiring proper setup before use. Despite some confusion in descriptions identifying it as an operating system, user account, or virtualization platform, the consistent narrative across multiple sources confirms x2openEuler as a migration-focused software suite. It is developed under the openEuler project, often associated with Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd., and functions as a critical system management tool within enterprise Linux deployment and upgrade workflows.<SEP>x2openEuler is a migration tool used to upgrade CentOS 7.6 to openEuler 20.03 LTS-SP1."}
