{"entity": "Upgrade", "type": "method", "description": "Upgrade refers to the critical process of updating a system, commonly involving the transition from CentOS 7.8.2003 to openEuler 20.03 LTS SP1, or more broadly, from CentOS to Euler. This includes the comprehensive update of the operating system, involving software, firmware, and system components such as directories, configuration files, and package management. The upgrade process encompasses multiple phases, including pre-checks, execution, and post-scripts, and is consistent with established migration procedures. It is recognized as the core operation step in the migration process and is generally scheduled to occur after environmental checks and is currently pending execution in some contexts. In certain environments, it is also accessible via a dropdown menu option in the development interface to initiate project-specific upgrades.\n\nMultiple sources indicate that the upgrade process can encounter failures, either during execution or due to unresolved dependencies, such as issues with bond0 failing to initialize properly post-upgrade. These failures may stem from incomplete or corrupted updates, with specific reports noting the process failing at the 95% completion stage. The upgrade failure has also been linked to potential disruptions in system accessibility and changes in system metrics. In one instance, the upgrade is described as the second step in the process that failed during execution, while in another, it is identified as the third phase that is still pending.\n\nThe upgrade process is not limited to operating system-level changes but also includes updating software packages and system configurations. It may involve updating specific components such as epkg to a newer version, indicating domain-specific applications of the term. Despite ongoing efforts, the overall process remains dependent on successful preconditions and error-free execution, with various factors influencing its success or failure, including compatibility, dependency resolution, and configuration integrity. The term 'Upgrade' thus denotes a multifaceted, system-critical operation central to modern system maintenance, currently in a pending or partially completed state across several instances.<SEP>The upgrade process involves updating system software or firmware, including rebuilding boot components.<SEP>Upgrade refers to the process of updating firmware, requiring adequate space on the Flash Chip."}
