{"entity": "QuickIssue", "type": "organization", "description": "QuickIssue is a system for reporting and tracking software issues.<SEP>QuickIssue is a dedicated issue tracking service developed for the openEuler Community in 2022 to improve accessibility and performance of issue submission and management.<SEP>QuickIssue is a tool developed for the openEuler community that enables users to search and manage issues and pull requests with filtering capabilities.<SEP>QuickIssue is a tool within the openEuler ecosystem that allows users to manage and track issues and pull requests, with recent updates enabling user login functionality.<SEP>QuickIssue is a tool for managing and tracking issues within the OpenEuler project.<SEP>QuickIssue is a tool hosted at https://quickissue.openeuler.org that helps users filter and manage pull requests assigned to them.<SEP>QuickIssue is a platform used by openEuler for tracking and managing technical issues reported by community members."}
{"entity": "myeuler", "type": "person", "description": "myeuler is a user and maintainers in the openEuler project who actively participates in the openEuler forum and related technical discussions. Through multiple contributions, myeuler provides technical guidance and insights on various aspects of the openEuler operating system. One of myeuler’s key roles involves addressing user concerns regarding system compatibility, notably commenting on the incompatibility between openEuler and CentOS, and expressing that integration of EX-NDE is not currently available, while encouraging community enthusiasts to contribute by porting it to openEuler. Additionally, myeuler has questioned the rationale of installing CentOS packages on openEuler, emphasizing the importance of using platform-appropriate software to ensure system stability and security.\n\nmyeuler also offers recommendations on deployment practices, most notably advising against using non-LTS (Long-Term Support) versions of openEuler in production environments due to their limited lifecycle and support. This reflects a broader concern for system reliability and long-term maintainability. The user has contributed insights into the future development roadmap of openEuler, including the planned support for .NET, and has discussed technical compatibility issues such as those surrounding Docker integration, indicating active engagement with both community and infrastructure challenges.\n\nFurthermore, myeuler has demonstrated a strategic understanding of openEuler’s growth, attributing the active development of its kernel to widespread corporate adoption and the presence of skilled developers within the ecosystem. The user has also encouraged developers to report issues via structured channels such as QuickIssue, indicating a role in guiding user reporting and consistent project communication. Overall, myeuler functions as a credible contributor and technical steward within the openEuler community, combining guidance, clarification, and forward-looking commentary.<SEP>myeuler is a user who acknowledged openEuler’s current focus on enterprise (2B) use while encouraging community-driven enhancements in usability.<SEP>myeuler is a user who commented positively on the book, praising it as a high-quality work by \"Zengqingfeng\".<SEP>myeuler is a user who provided a suggestion to report the issue on QuickIssue for tracking by developers.<SEP>myeuler is a user who contributed a post in the discussion thread regarding how to find community project SIGs within the openEuler ecosystem.<SEP>myeuler is a user who suggested contributing work back to the community for broader adoption of i3wm.<SEP>myeuler is a contributor who replied that no desktop version of OpenEuler is currently available but referred to installing DDE, UKUI, or Gnome."}
