{"entity": "OpenEuler 24.03", "type": "distribution", "description": "OpenEuler 24.03 is a specific version of the OpenEuler operating system, released as part of the Long-Term Support – Server Operating System (LTS-SOS) series, designed for enterprise and server environments. It is a Linux-based open-source operating system developed to support enterprise workloads, software development tasks, and containerized applications, with a strong emphasis on system stability, long-term maintainability, and security. The distribution supports multiple hardware architectures, including aarch64 and RISC-V, and has been deployed on various platforms such as the ThinkPad T580 and ThinkPad T490 for operations like system partition cloning, boot configuration, and cross-device data transfer. OpenEuler 24.03 facilitates advanced system management tasks, including network port configuration, system updates, and the installation of critical software components such as OpenLDAP, CUPS (Common Unix Printing System), Filebeat, ignition-fuel-tools, and the OpenSSH 9.8 RPM package. Remote desktop access is supported via xrdp; however, users have consistently reported recurring black screen issues during login attempts, suggesting potential instability in the remote access stack.\n\nDespite its robust architecture and enhanced package repository availability compared to earlier versions, OpenEuler 24.03 faces notable compatibility and stability challenges in specific deployment scenarios. A well-documented and consistently reported issue involves instability when using the Wangxun network card on the aarch64 architecture, confirmed across multiple independent deployments. This problem significantly impacts network reliability and is a key consideration for system administrators planning to use this configuration. Additionally, the graphical desktop environment contains usability bugs that may impair desktop-centric workflows. On certain hardware, such as the ThinkPad T580, users have observed erratic mouse cursor behavior shortly after login, suggesting possible deficiencies in input driver handling or display management. Installation failures have also been reported when deploying OpenEuler 24.03 via InCloud Sphere V5, with recurring error messages persisting in specific workflows, indicating systemic issues in certain virtualization environments.\n\nThese challenges suggest that while OpenEuler 24.03 remains a capable, enterprise-grade Linux distribution with strong support for modern hardware and containerized workloads, its deployment requires careful evaluation—particularly in environments involving the Wangxun network card, graphical interface use, or remote desktop access. Users are advised to implement mitigation strategies and conduct thorough testing prior to production use, especially when integrating with specific hardware or virtualization platforms.<SEP>OpenEuler 24.03 is a Linux distribution that experiences instability when using the Wangxun network card on aarch64 architecture."}
