{"entity": "openEuler", "type": "organization", "description": "openEuler is an open-source, community-driven Linux distribution developed and maintained by the OpenEuler Community under the OpenAtom Foundation. Originally initiated by Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd. as a successor to EulerOS, the project has evolved into a globally significant open-source operating system platform focused on enterprise stability, security, and performance. It is designed for diverse computing environments including servers, cloud infrastructure, edge computing, and embedded systems, with strong support for critical sectors such as government, finance, transportation, energy, and education. openEuler supports multiple processor architectures, including x86_64, aarch64 (ARM64), RISC-V, Kunpeng, Phytium, and Zen, promoting an inclusive, architecture-aware software ecosystem.\n\nThe project provides long-term support (LTS) versions such as openEuler 20.03 LTS (with SP1, SP3), openEuler 22.03 LTS (SP1, SP2, SP3), and openEuler 24.03 LTS (with SP2), each offering extended security patches, kernel updates, and comprehensive release notes. These LTS releases ensure reliability for production and enterprise deployments, making openEuler a leading choice for new server operating systems in China, with over 3 million installations and a substantial market share. It serves as a key migration target for legacy systems such as CentOS 7.4 and CentOS 8.2, supported by tools like x2openEuler, and is used to build cloud server images, virtual machines (VMs), containers (via iSulad), and environments like WSL on Microsoft Store.\n\nopenEuler offers a robust software stack with support for essential system components including the SysMaster initialization system, GRUB boot loader with default credentials (root: openEuler#12), and NetworkManager for network configuration. The platform uses DNF and YUM as primary package managers and maintains official software repositories with RPM packages, including mirrors from Huawei Cloud and Tsinghua University. GPG key signing and secure package verification ensure system integrity, while tools like createrepo and Reproducible Builds (adopted since 2022) enhance trust and transparency. The project includes advanced frameworks such as secGear for confidential computing, the Gazelle networking stack, and initiatives like Euler-Copilot, A-Ops, and gala-ops to support system automation and deployment.\n\nSecurity is prioritized through SELinux policies, PAM_wheel.so enforcement, a dedicated security committee, and the publication of secure configuration benchmarks. openEuler supports a wide array of applications, including Apache, MySQL, Zabbix, ROS2 Humble, NVIDIA graphics drivers, openGauss, QGIS, and distributed soft bus systems, while also supporting kernel development, driver compilation, AI inference, and big data processing. It is compatible with popular software stacks and deployment environments, including Kubernetes via Eggops and containerized workloads via iSulad.\n\nDespite some technical limitations—such as missing default Docker packages in certain versions, lack of Python 2 support from 22.03 LTS onward, restricted partitioning during installation, and incompatibility with software like Oracle, ZeroTier, Mellanox OFED, and Cloudera Manager Agent—openEuler continues to expand its ecosystem. Challenges related to SSH, GRUB, and network connectivity have been reported but are actively addressed. The project thrives through community collaboration via Special Interest Groups (SIGs), public forums, documentation, and transparent governance. It plays a central role in China’s national innovation (xinchuang) initiative and has gained international attention through global outreach, conferences, and recognition on platforms like DistroWatch, underscoring its impact as a unified, open, and future-ready operating system.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source community-driven operating system project with a focus on stability, performance, and security.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project aimed at providing a stable and secure platform for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project that provides a stable and secure platform for enterprise applications.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project that provides application images and supports various software components."}
